From: =?UTF-8?q?Bedirhan=20Gen=C3=A7aslan?=
 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:17:58 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 56/71] Delete the history-time shrinker the source-side one
 replaced

Shrinking moved to `to_response_item`, where the trimmed text is the only
version the prefix ever holds. The old pass over history stayed behind it,
still called from `for_prompt_annotated`, still gated on `prefix_break` --
and that gate is the reason it can never do anything now: the output it would
trim has already been trimmed on the way in, so there is nothing left in
history to find. The recorded sessions agree: `shrunk_tokens_removed` and
`shrinkable_before_break` are 0 across 117 requests of real work.

Keeping it was not free. Rewriting output the cache has already seen is the
one thing this design exists to avoid, so a dead path that would do exactly
that is a trap, and its always-zero counters made the sidecar and
`suffice debug transcript` report a mechanism that had not run.

`shrink_exec_output` and the allowlist stay; the tests that cover them --
the allowlist, the size floor, the marker, the argv shapes -- now run through
the live path. The transcript still flags a trimmed output, reading the marker
out of the text rather than a sidecar column, which also makes it work on
rollouts recorded before the sidecar existed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
